      "dom_id": "question-15784",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $u=1-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|).\n$$",
